Chapter: CapMediaFamilies

 Camera, Microphone, Location, Sensors and Display are effects the
 foreword declares, and until now not one of them could appear in a
 program's row: they were absent from capability-vocabulary, so
 `opening : [Camera] ...` was CDX4001, "no capability the manifest can
 carry". They are carriable capabilities now -- a bit each, a cap id
 each, the same shape as Gpu or Identity -- while their operations stay
 declared and not yet performable. This test asks
 one question of all five at once: does the capability
 survive the manifest and reach the granted process word, or is it
 silently dropped somewhere between the row and the boot grant?

 Each family declares no direction (no .Read / .Write member), so it is
 a single bit: camera is 19, microphone 20, location 21, sensors 22,
 display 23. The bits are read through process-get-cap, which is the
 granted word the kernel itself tests before a syscall -- not a
 restatement of the source. A 1 means the name agreed all the way from
 capability-vocabulary through manifest-cap-id to boot-cap-mask-for. A 0
 means it was known in one table and lost in another -- exactly the
 failure 1.13 is about, and the reason all three tables are exercised
 here rather than trusted.

Section: Bits

  cap-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bit (word) (n) = if bit-and word (bit-shl 1 n) /= 0 then 1 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Camera, Microphone, Location, Sensors, Display] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("camera "     & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 19))
    print-line-uni ("microphone " & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 20))
    print-line-uni ("location "   & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 21))
    print-line-uni ("sensors "    & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 22))
    print-line-uni ("display "    & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 23))
  end
